<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Satraps' Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 372–362 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Satraps' Revolt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satraps</a>' Revolt was a series of uprisings by provincial governors in the western parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> in c. 372–362 BCE, under Artaxerxes II. It ultimately failed, largely through betrayals among the rebels, but it exposed the tensions between the king and his powerful satraps.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-satraps-revolt.jpg" alt="Great Satraps' Revolt" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DPhilipAllen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:360s_Satrap_Revolt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> who revolted against the Great King Artaxerxes II were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datames">Datames</a>, Ariobarzanes, Orontes, Autophradates and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a>, who rose at different times and for different reasons. Though often portrayed as a general uprising, the revolt had little coordination; the rebels never directly threatened Artaxerxes and aimed at secession rather than a takeover of the empire. Datames, who had inherited a minor satrapy in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from his father Camissares, had won campaigns against the Cardusii, Thyus of Paphlagonia and Aspis of Cataonia. Leading the king's army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, he learned that jealous courtiers were speaking against him, abandoned his command and raised an army in Paphlagonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datames">Datames</a> defeated the Pisidians sent against him and fought Autophradates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> to a stalemate, but at the king's prompting Mithradates, son of Ariobarzanes, won his trust and killed him at a meeting. In 366 Ariobarzanes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, refused to give up his satrapy to Artabazus, son of his brother Pharnabazus and the king's daughter Apame. Facing Autophradates and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a> of Caria, while Cotys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> attacked Sestos, he paid the Spartan king Agesilaus and gave Crithote and Sestos to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> for the help of Timotheus, and Athens made him a citizen.</p><p>Orontes, son of Artasuras and husband of Artaxerxes' daughter Rhodogunê, had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 401. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> says the peoples who had revolted, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycians">Lycians</a>, Pisidians, Pamphylians, Cilicians, Syrians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, chose him as their general. With funds to pay twenty thousand men for a year he hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, fought off a siege at Mt. Tmolus, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, and routed Autophradates at Cyme. Autophradates himself at some point joined the rebels, according to Diodorus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The revolt ended in betrayal. Ariobarzanes was betrayed by his son to Artaxerxes, who had him crucified, and Orontes arrested the rebel leaders and delivered them to the king, regaining his favour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a>, whose part is known only from the list of Diodorus and from the attack on Ariobarzanes, which he broke off at the urging of Agesilaus, was reconciled with the king and held his position until his death in 353. Autophradates was reinstated and in the 350s was sent against Eubulus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atarneus">Atarneus</a>, giving up the siege, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> reports, when Eubulus offered to leave for less than it would cost. A passage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a> of 354 has led some to suppose that Orontes rebelled again, but nothing confirms it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Satraps' Revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
